# neutron-zzz7-wic-readthrough  (env NEUTRON_WIC_READTHROUGH=1 -- OFF BY DEFAULT)
#
# WHAT THE USER NOTICES
#   Premiere's timeline is choppy when zooming. After the heap and font-cache fixes landed,
#   the single largest remaining cost on the UI thread was not drawing at all -- it was
#   MEMORY COPYING: `ucrtbase!memmove` at 29% of the thread.
#
# WHERE IT COMES FROM
#   Premiere draws its interface in small pieces, each rendered by Direct2D onto the graphics
#   card and then handed back as a WIC bitmap. Wine copied every finished piece down from the
#   card into that WIC bitmap, and Premiere immediately copied it out again into its own
#   buffer. The WIC bitmap in the middle was pure overhead. Measured over 30 s of timeline
#   zooming: 20.5 GB down, then essentially all of it straight back out again.
#
#   Windows does not pay this. There, a WIC bitmap render target is a SOFTWARE target --
#   Direct2D renders straight into the bitmap's own memory and there is no round trip at all.
#   Wine always renders on the GPU, so the readback exists only here. That makes it a Wine
#   bug in the sense that matters: same binary, same work, extra cost.
#
# THE FIX
#   present() maps the staging texture as it always did, but instead of copying through the
#   WIC bitmap it ATTACHES the mapping to it (private interface, see wine/neutron_wicgpu.h).
#   CopyPixels then serves the app straight out of that mapping. One copy instead of two.
#
# WHY IT IS SAFE -- the two rules the design is built around
#   1. NO D3D OFF THE DRAWING THREAD. This project already killed one freeze class caused by
#      touching D3D from threads that otherwise never do. Every D3D call (Map, Unmap, texture
#      lifetime) stays on presenting threads: the bitmap's release callback only QUEUES, and
#      the next present drains the queue. A handoff also holds a reference on the factory, so
#      a bitmap outliving the factory cannot queue onto a freed list.
#   2. THE PIXELS OUTLIVE THE READER. The render-target pool recycles targets between bitmaps,
#      so present GIVES THE TEXTURE AWAY and takes another from a small pool. The texture
#      follows the data and a later frame can never overwrite pixels somebody is still
#      waiting to read. Anything that cannot be served this way -- a write lock, an exotic
#      shape, the outstanding-handoff cap, a non-Wine windowscodecs -- MATERIALISES and falls
#      back to exactly the old code. Correctness never depends on the fast path being taken.
#
# WHAT DECIDED IT WAS WORTH BUILDING (probe: patches/diagnostic/neutron-wicprobe.mypatch)
#   Three things could have killed the design; all three were measured first, in one 30 s run:
#     read on the thread that drew   100.0%  (25,954 / 25,954)  <- the fatal one, and it passed
#     read covers the whole image    100.0%
#     matching row spacing           100.0%  of the app's own reads
#     only one read per drawn frame   99.8%
#     app reads per download          1.006  (17,025 reads / 16,927 downloads)
#   Premiere reads every downloaded bitmap exactly once, in full, on the drawing thread, and
#   never touches it again. A textbook removable intermediate.
#
# VERIFICATION -- preserved-fixes/harnesses/wicrt.c, run BOTH ways, 227 pixel checks each:
#     NEUTRON_WIC_READTHROUGH=0  ->  227 checks, 0 failures
#     NEUTRON_WIC_READTHROUGH=1  ->  227 checks, 0 failures   (CAP HIT x32, declined=0)
#   It compares pixel VALUES, not survival: the failure mode here is a bitmap quietly showing
#   the WRONG FRAME, which any "did it crash" test passes with a completely broken build.
#   The test that matters is interleave(): draw A, draw B, THEN read A. A naive deferred
#   download hands A the texture B has since drawn over and A silently returns B's pixels.
#
# ⚠️ THE FIRST PREMIERE RUN FAILED, AND THE TWO BUGS ARE WORTH KEEPING WRITTEN DOWN.
# Harness-green is not app-green: both bugs needed a real workload to show up at all.
#
#  1. THE FALLBACK PATH DOUBLE-COPIED. When a handoff is refused, present falls back to the
#     old Lock(WRITE) path -- and the Lock hook dutifully MATERIALISED the previous frame
#     into a buffer present was about to overwrite completely. A whole wasted copy on top of
#     the copy the fallback already does, so the moment the cap was reached the fix cost
#     MORE than it saved. present() now detaches without materialising first.
#
#  2. THE CAP WAS SIZED FROM A WRONG MENTAL MODEL. A mapping is released when the bitmap is
#     REDRAWN or DESTROYED -- not when it is read. So the outstanding set is the number of
#     LIVE render-target bitmaps, not the number waiting to be read, and Premiere keeps far
#     more alive than "one read per frame, arriving immediately" suggested. A 32-frame cap
#     saturated after 64 presents and then fell back 14,278 times in one 30 s run.
#     The quantity actually worth bounding is MEMORY, so the limit is now a byte budget
#     (NEUTRON_WIC_READTHROUGH_MB, default 192) with a generous count backstop -- and the
#     PEAK working set is logged, so the next sizing decision comes from a measurement.
#
# ⚠️ OFF BY DEFAULT. It changes who owns rendered pixels and when they are copied; it gets the
# same soak-behind-a-switch rollout the large-block heap cache got. Turn it on with
# NEUTRON_WIC_READTHROUGH=1.
#
# ⚠️ DIAGNOSTICS PRINT AT POWERS OF TWO, then every 512. A plain modulo prints NOTHING on a
# short run, which is indistinguishable from the code never executing -- that misread this
# path twice during development, and the cap-fallback path looked dead when it was firing 32
# times just past the last sampling boundary. Rare events (cap hit, declined) print every time.
diff --git a/include/wine/neutron_wicgpu.h b/include/wine/neutron_wicgpu.h
new file mode 100644
index 00000000000..89123f5dba1
--- /dev/null
+++ b/include/wine/neutron_wicgpu.h
@@ -0,0 +1,105 @@
+/*
+ * NEUTRON: letting a WIC bitmap serve its pixels straight out of GPU-resident memory.
+ *
+ * THE PROBLEM, in plain terms
+ * --------------------------
+ * When Premiere draws a piece of its interface, Direct2D renders it on the graphics
+ * card. Wine then copies the result down into a WIC bitmap, and Premiere immediately
+ * copies it again out of that bitmap into its own buffer. The WIC bitmap in the middle
+ * is pure overhead: measured over 30 seconds of timeline zooming, 20.5 GB goes down into
+ * it and essentially all of it straight back out, and `memmove` is 29% of the UI thread.
+ *
+ * Measured on 2026-07-30 (windowscodecs probe, Premiere 2025, GPU UI mode):
+ *
+ *     app reads per download            1.006      (17,025 reads / 16,927 downloads)
+ *     read on the thread that drew      100.0%     (25,954 / 25,954)
+ *     read covers the whole image       100.0%
+ *     read uses the same row spacing    100.0%     (of the app's own reads)
+ *     only one read per drawn frame      99.8%
+ *
+ * So every downloaded bitmap is read exactly once, in full, at matching stride, on the
+ * drawing thread, and is then never touched again. It is as pure an intermediate as it
+ * gets, and the middle copy can be removed.
+ *
+ * HOW
+ * ---
+ * d2d1's present() maps the staging texture as it already does, but instead of copying
+ * the pixels into the WIC bitmap it ATTACHES the mapping to the bitmap through the
+ * interface below. windowscodecs then serves CopyPixels directly out of that mapping.
+ * One copy instead of two.
+ *
+ * TWO RULES THIS CONTRACT EXISTS TO ENFORCE
+ * -----------------------------------------
+ * 1. NO D3D OFF THE DRAWING THREAD. This project has already killed one freeze class
+ *    caused by touching D3D from threads that otherwise never do. So d2d1 keeps every
+ *    D3D call (Map, Unmap, texture lifetime) on presenting threads: release() below is
+ *    explicitly allowed to be called from any thread and must therefore do no D3D work
+ *    itself -- it queues, and the next present drains the queue.
+ *
+ * 2. THE PIXELS MUST OUTLIVE THE READER. The render-target pool recycles staging
+ *    textures between bitmaps, so a bitmap that still owes someone its pixels cannot be
+ *    left pointing at a texture that has been handed to the next frame. d2d1 therefore
+ *    gives the texture AWAY to the bitmap and takes a fresh one; the texture follows the
+ *    data, and reuse can never overwrite a pending read.
+ *
+ * A bitmap that cannot serve a request out of the mapping (an odd sub-rect, a write
+ * lock, a pixel-format query that needs real memory) MATERIALISES first: it performs the
+ * copy it had deferred, into its own buffer, and drops the source. Correctness never
+ * depends on the fast path being taken, which is also why QueryInterface failing -- for
+ * example against a native windowscodecs -- simply falls back to the old behaviour.
+ */
+
+#ifndef __WINE_NEUTRON_WICGPU_H
+#define __WINE_NEUTRON_WICGPU_H
+
+/* Callbacks supplied by the pixel producer (d2d1). All are called with the bitmap's own
+ * lock held, so they must not call back into the bitmap. */
+struct neutron_wicgpu_source
+{
+    /* Serve a CopyPixels request straight from the mapping. Return S_FALSE to decline
+     * (the bitmap then materialises and serves it from its own memory instead), any
+     * failure HRESULT to fail the call. */
+    HRESULT (CDECL *copy_pixels)(void *ctx, UINT bpp, UINT width, UINT height,
+            const WICRect *rc, UINT dst_stride, UINT dst_size, BYTE *dst);
+
+    /* Perform the deferred download into the bitmap's own buffer. Called at most once
+     * per attached source, immediately before the source is released. */
+    HRESULT (CDECL *materialize)(void *ctx, BYTE *dst, UINT dst_stride,
+            UINT height, UINT row_bytes);
+
+    /* The bitmap is finished with this source. MAY BE CALLED FROM ANY THREAD, including
+     * during bitmap destruction -- see rule 1 above: do no D3D work here. */
+    void (CDECL *release)(void *ctx);
+};
+
+/* Private interface, exposed by Wine's own windowscodecs BitmapImpl via QueryInterface.
+ * {6e657574-726f-6e01-77-69-63-67-70-75-30-31} -- "neutron" + "wicgpu01". */
+DEFINE_GUID(IID_INeutronWicGpuBitmap, 0x6e657574,0x726f,0x6e01,0x77,0x69,0x63,0x67,0x70,0x75,0x30,0x31);
+
+typedef struct INeutronWicGpuBitmap INeutronWicGpuBitmap;
+
+typedef struct INeutronWicGpuBitmapVtbl
+{
+    HRESULT (STDMETHODCALLTYPE *QueryInterface)(INeutronWicGpuBitmap *iface, REFIID iid, void **out);
+    ULONG   (STDMETHODCALLTYPE *AddRef)(INeutronWicGpuBitmap *iface);
+    ULONG   (STDMETHODCALLTYPE *Release)(INeutronWicGpuBitmap *iface);
+
+    /* Attach a GPU-resident source, replacing (and releasing) any source already
+     * attached. Passing a NULL source detaches without materialising -- only correct
+     * when the caller is about to overwrite every pixel, which is exactly what a
+     * full-surface present does. Returns S_OK when the source was taken. */
+    HRESULT (STDMETHODCALLTYPE *SetSource)(INeutronWicGpuBitmap *iface,
+            const struct neutron_wicgpu_source *ops, void *ctx);
+} INeutronWicGpuBitmapVtbl;
+
+struct INeutronWicGpuBitmap
+{
+    const INeutronWicGpuBitmapVtbl *lpVtbl;
+};
+
+#define INeutronWicGpuBitmap_QueryInterface(p,a,b) (p)->lpVtbl->QueryInterface(p,a,b)
+#define INeutronWicGpuBitmap_AddRef(p)             (p)->lpVtbl->AddRef(p)
+#define INeutronWicGpuBitmap_Release(p)            (p)->lpVtbl->Release(p)
+#define INeutronWicGpuBitmap_SetSource(p,a,b)      (p)->lpVtbl->SetSource(p,a,b)
+
+#endif /* __WINE_NEUTRON_WICGPU_H */
diff --git a/dlls/d2d1/d2d1_private.h b/dlls/d2d1/d2d1_private.h
index 9c78aaf92e2..cb227dd80d5 100644
--- a/dlls/d2d1/d2d1_private.h
+++ b/dlls/d2d1/d2d1_private.h
@@ -745,14 +1241,102 @@ struct d2d_factory
 
     CRITICAL_SECTION cs;
     D2D1_FACTORY_TYPE factory_type;
+    /* NEUTRON WIC RENDER-TARGET POOL: Premiere creates ~29 WIC bitmap render
+     * targets PER FRAME and releases them again -- 4.78ms/frame in creation
+     * alone, and each one also brings its own EndDraw GPU round trip (7.25ms
+     * more), together 20% of a 59ms frame. Creating one costs ~167us: a device
+     * context init (~65-88us), a CPU-readable STAGING texture whose cost scales
+     * with area (15-76us), plus the D3D texture and DXGI surface. None of that
+     * depends on WHICH IWICBitmap is wrapped -- only on size and format -- so a
+     * released target is kept here and handed back to the next request of the
+     * same shape, with its drawing state reset. */
+    /* NEUTRON BATCHED DEVICE-CONTEXT STATE: which render target's ID3DDeviceContextState
+     * is currently installed on the shared immediate context, and the state that was
+     * installed before d2d first took over. Guarded by cs (the same lock the draw path
+     * already takes). See d2d_device_context_install_state(). */
+    ID3DDeviceContextState *d3d_current_state;
+    ID3DDeviceContextState *d3d_app_state;
+
+    struct list wic_rt_pool;
+    unsigned int wic_rt_pool_size;
+
+    /* NEUTRON WIC READ-THROUGH (env NEUTRON_WIC_READTHROUGH=1): rather than downloading
+     * a rendered bitmap into WIC memory and letting the app copy it out again, present()
+     * hands the staging texture's mapping to the WIC bitmap and the app is served
+     * straight from it -- one copy instead of two. See wine/neutron_wicgpu.h.
+     *
+     * The texture goes WITH the pixels, because the render-target pool recycles targets
+     * between bitmaps and must never overwrite a frame somebody is still waiting to
+     * read. So present() gives its staging texture away and takes another:
+     *   wic_gpu_recycle  -- handoffs the reader has finished with. Queued from ANY
+     *                       thread, so nothing here may touch D3D; the next present
+     *                       drains it (unmapping on a thread that already owns D3D).
+     *   wic_staging_pool -- drained textures, unmapped and ready to be taken again.
+     * Both guarded by cs. */
+    struct list wic_gpu_recycle;
+    struct list wic_staging_pool;
+    unsigned int wic_staging_pool_size;
+    unsigned int wic_gpu_outstanding;
+    SIZE_T wic_gpu_outstanding_bytes;
 };
 
+#define D2D_WIC_RT_POOL_MAX 24
+/* An attached mapping is released when the bitmap is redrawn or destroyed -- NOT when it
+ * is read. So the outstanding set is the number of LIVE render-target bitmaps, not the
+ * number waiting to be read, and Premiere keeps far more of those alive than the first
+ * estimate assumed. A count-based cap of 32 saturated after 64 presents and then fell
+ * back 14,278 times.
+ *
+ * The thing actually worth bounding is MEMORY (each handoff pins one staging texture), so
+ * the real limit is bytes; the count is only a backstop against pathological tiny
+ * bitmaps. Both are deliberately generous, and the peak is logged so the next sizing
+ * decision is made from a measurement rather than another guess. */
+#define D2D_WIC_GPU_MAX_OUTSTANDING 512
+#define D2D_WIC_STAGING_POOL_MAX 64
+
+/* Memory budget for pixels waiting to be read, MB. Override: NEUTRON_WIC_READTHROUGH_MB. */
+static inline SIZE_T neutron_wic_readthrough_bytes(void)
+{
+    static SIZE_T v;
+    if (!v)
+    {
+        char buf[16];
+        unsigned mb = 0;
+        if (GetEnvironmentVariableA("NEUTRON_WIC_READTHROUGH_MB", buf, sizeof(buf)))
+            mb = strtoul(buf, NULL, 10);
+        if (!mb) mb = 192;
+        v = (SIZE_T)mb << 20;
+    }
+    return v;
+}
+
+/* NEUTRON WIC READ-THROUGH: off by default. It changes who owns rendered pixels and when
+ * they are copied, which is worth soaking behind a switch before it becomes the default
+ * -- the same rollout the large-block heap cache got. */
+static inline BOOL neutron_wic_readthrough(void)
+{
+    static int v = -1;
+    if (v < 0)
+    {
+        char buf[8];
+        v = (GetEnvironmentVariableA("NEUTRON_WIC_READTHROUGH", buf, sizeof(buf)) && buf[0] == '1') ? 1 : 0;
+    }
+    return v;
+}
+
 static inline struct d2d_factory *unsafe_impl_from_ID2D1Factory(ID2D1Factory *iface)
 {
     return CONTAINING_RECORD(iface, struct d2d_factory, ID2D1Factory7_iface);
 }
 
 void d2d_effects_init_builtins(struct d2d_factory *factory);
+/* NEUTRON WIC RENDER-TARGET POOL */
+struct d2d_wic_render_target *d2d_wic_render_target_pool_lookup(struct d2d_factory *factory,
+        IWICBitmap *bitmap, const D2D1_RENDER_TARGET_PROPERTIES *desc);
+void d2d_factory_drain_wic_rt_pool(struct d2d_factory *factory);
+/* NEUTRON WIC READ-THROUGH: unmap and release everything still queued or pooled. */
+void d2d_factory_drain_wic_gpu(struct d2d_factory *factory);
+void d2d_device_context_release_state(struct d2d_device_context *context);
 HRESULT d2d_factory_create_device(ID2D1Factory1 *factory, IDXGIDevice *dxgi_device,
         bool allow_get_dxgi_device, REFIID iid, void **device);
 struct d2d_effect_registration * d2d_factory_get_registered_effect(ID2D1Factory *factory,
diff --git a/dlls/d2d1/factory.c b/dlls/d2d1/factory.c
index 80c147638cd..b7439f6ec3c 100644
--- a/dlls/d2d1/factory.c
+++ b/dlls/d2d1/factory.c
@@ -156,6 +159,8 @@ static ULONG STDMETHODCALLTYPE d2d_factory_Release(ID2D1Factory7 *iface)
         {
             d2d_effect_registration_cleanup(reg);
         }
+        d2d_factory_drain_wic_rt_pool(factory);   /* NEUTRON WIC RT POOL */
+        d2d_factory_drain_wic_gpu(factory);       /* NEUTRON WIC READ-THROUGH */
         DeleteCriticalSection(&factory->cs);
         free(factory);
     }
@@ -1360,6 +1463,11 @@ static void d2d_factory_init(struct d2d_factory *factory, D2D1_FACTORY_TYPE fact
     factory->refcount = 1;
     d2d_factory_reload_sysmetrics(factory);
     list_init(&factory->effects);
+    list_init(&factory->wic_rt_pool);   /* NEUTRON WIC RT POOL */
+    list_init(&factory->wic_gpu_recycle);    /* NEUTRON WIC READ-THROUGH */
+    list_init(&factory->wic_staging_pool);
+    factory->wic_staging_pool_size = 0;
+    factory->wic_gpu_outstanding = 0;
     InitializeCriticalSection(&factory->cs);
     InitOnceInitialize(&factory->init_builtins);
 }
diff --git a/dlls/d2d1/wic_render_target.c b/dlls/d2d1/wic_render_target.c
index c0720e69a8a..e2ee0037150 100644
--- a/dlls/d2d1/wic_render_target.c
+++ b/dlls/d2d1/wic_render_target.c
@@ -19,14 +19,398 @@
 #include "d2d1_private.h"
 #include "initguid.h"
 #include "wincodec.h"
+#include "wine/neutron_wicgpu.h"
 
 WINE_DEFAULT_DEBUG_CHANNEL(d2d);
+WINE_DECLARE_DEBUG_CHANNEL(nwicgpu);
 
 static inline struct d2d_wic_render_target *impl_from_IUnknown(IUnknown *iface)
 {
     return CONTAINING_RECORD(iface, struct d2d_wic_render_target, IUnknown_iface);
 }
 
+/* ---- NEUTRON WIC READ-THROUGH ----------------------------------------------
+ * See wine/neutron_wicgpu.h for what this is and the measurements behind it. The short
+ * version: present() used to copy the whole rendered image out of the staging texture
+ * into the WIC bitmap, and the app then copied it out of the WIC bitmap into its own
+ * buffer. Premiere reads every one of those bitmaps exactly once, in full, on the thread
+ * that drew it -- so the middle copy is removable. present() now hands the mapping over
+ * and the app is served directly from it.
+ *
+ * The staging texture travels WITH the pixels (present takes a fresh one), because the
+ * render-target pool recycles targets between bitmaps and must never overwrite a frame
+ * that has not been read yet. */
+struct d2d_wic_gpu_handoff
+{
+    struct list entry;
+    struct d2d_factory *factory;
+    /* A WIC bitmap may outlive the factory that rendered into it. The handoff therefore
+     * keeps the factory alive for as long as it exists -- without this, a bitmap released
+     * after the app dropped its last factory reference would queue itself onto a freed
+     * list and dereference a freed handoff. */
+    ID2D1Factory1 *factory_iface;
+    ID3D10Texture2D *texture;
+    void *data;                     /* the live mapping */
+    unsigned int row_pitch;
+    SIZE_T bytes;                   /* what this handoff pins, for the memory budget */
+    /* bpp here is BYTES per pixel (as d2d_wic_render_target counts it); windowscodecs
+     * passes BITS. The conversion happens once, in copy_pixels below. */
+    unsigned int width, height, bpp;
+};
+
+/* A staging texture that has been unmapped and can be handed to the next present. */
+struct d2d_wic_staging
+{
+    struct list entry;
+    ID3D10Texture2D *texture;
+    ID3D10Device *device;           /* borrowed: compared only, kept alive by texture */
+    unsigned int width, height;
+    DXGI_FORMAT format;
+};
+
+static LONG nwg_handed, nwg_fallback_cap, nwg_fallback_noiface, nwg_staging_hit, nwg_staging_new;
+/* Peak working set, for sizing the budget from evidence rather than from a guess. Process
+ * globals rather than per-factory: this is a diagnostic, and one number across every
+ * factory is the one worth reading. */
+static unsigned nwg_peak_count;
+static SIZE_T nwg_peak_bytes;
+
+static void d2d_wic_gpu_report(void)
+{
+    LONG n = nwg_handed + nwg_fallback_cap + nwg_fallback_noiface;
+
+    if (!TRACE_ON(nwicgpu)) return;
+    /* Powers of two, then every 512. A pure modulo prints NOTHING on a short run --
+     * indistinguishable from the path never executing, which is exactly how this first
+     * read against the harness. Powers of two give a handful of lines at any scale
+     * (1,2,4...512) and the modulo keeps running totals coming on a long one. */
+    if (n > 8 && (n & (n - 1)) && n % 512) return;
+    TRACE_(nwicgpu)("neutron-wicgpu(d2d): handed=%ld fallback_cap=%ld fallback_noiface=%ld "
+            "staging_reused=%ld staging_created=%ld peak=%u frames/%u MB\n",
+            nwg_handed, nwg_fallback_cap, nwg_fallback_noiface, nwg_staging_hit, nwg_staging_new,
+            nwg_peak_count, (unsigned)(nwg_peak_bytes >> 20));
+}
+
+/* Serve a CopyPixels request straight out of the mapping.
+ *
+ * Deliberately handles only the byte-aligned case. Everything Premiere does is 32bpp
+ * full-rect, and declining (S_FALSE) makes the bitmap materialise and run windowscodecs'
+ * own copy_pixels -- so exotic shapes stay on exactly the code that handled them before
+ * rather than on a second, subtly different implementation of the same rules. */
+static HRESULT CDECL d2d_wic_gpu_copy_pixels(void *ctx, UINT bpp, UINT width, UINT height,
+        const WICRect *rc, UINT dst_stride, UINT dst_size, BYTE *dst)
+{
+    struct d2d_wic_gpu_handoff *h = ctx;
+    unsigned int bytes_per_row, row;
+    const BYTE *src;
+    WICRect rect;
+
+    if (bpp % 8 || bpp / 8 != h->bpp || width != h->width || height != h->height)
+        return S_FALSE;
+
+    if (!rc)
+    {
+        rect.X = 0;
+        rect.Y = 0;
+        rect.Width = width;
+        rect.Height = height;
+        rc = &rect;
+    }
+    else if (rc->X < 0 || rc->Y < 0 || rc->X + rc->Width > (INT)width
+            || rc->Y + rc->Height > (INT)height)
+        return E_INVALIDARG;
+
+    bytes_per_row = (bpp * rc->Width + 7) / 8;
+    if (dst_stride < bytes_per_row)
+        return E_INVALIDARG;
+    if (dst_stride * (rc->Height - 1) + bytes_per_row > dst_size)
+        return E_INVALIDARG;
+
+    src = (const BYTE *)h->data + (size_t)h->row_pitch * rc->Y + (size_t)rc->X * h->bpp;
+    for (row = 0; row < (unsigned int)rc->Height; ++row)
+    {
+        memcpy(dst, src, bytes_per_row);
+        src += h->row_pitch;
+        dst += dst_stride;
+    }
+    return S_OK;
+}
+
+/* Perform the download that was deferred, into the bitmap's own buffer. */
+static HRESULT CDECL d2d_wic_gpu_materialize(void *ctx, BYTE *dst, UINT dst_stride,
+        UINT height, UINT row_bytes)
+{
+    struct d2d_wic_gpu_handoff *h = ctx;
+    const BYTE *src = h->data;
+    unsigned int i;
+
+    if (height > h->height) height = h->height;
+    if (row_bytes > h->row_pitch) row_bytes = h->row_pitch;
+
+    for (i = 0; i < height; ++i)
+    {
+        memcpy(dst, src, row_bytes);
+        src += h->row_pitch;
+        dst += dst_stride;
+    }
+    return S_OK;
+}
+
+/* CALLED FROM ANY THREAD -- including a bitmap being destroyed on a thread that has never
+ * touched D3D. Unmapping here would reintroduce exactly the off-thread-D3D freeze class
+ * this project already killed, so this only queues; the next present drains it. */
+static void CDECL d2d_wic_gpu_release(void *ctx)
+{
+    struct d2d_wic_gpu_handoff *h = ctx;
+    struct d2d_factory *factory = h->factory;
+    ID2D1Factory1 *factory_iface = h->factory_iface;
+
+    EnterCriticalSection(&factory->cs);
+    list_add_tail(&factory->wic_gpu_recycle, &h->entry);
+    LeaveCriticalSection(&factory->cs);
+    /* h is now owned by the queue and may already have been drained and freed -- do not
+     * touch it again. Dropping the factory reference last is what lets the factory be
+     * destroyed once nothing is outstanding; if this is the final reference its
+     * destructor drains the queue, which is the one moment unmapping happens off a
+     * presenting thread and is safe precisely because everything else is already gone. */
+    ID2D1Factory1_Release(factory_iface);
+}
+
+static const struct neutron_wicgpu_source d2d_wic_gpu_ops =
+{
+    d2d_wic_gpu_copy_pixels,
+    d2d_wic_gpu_materialize,
+    d2d_wic_gpu_release,
+};
+
+static void d2d_wic_gpu_put_staging(struct d2d_factory *factory, ID3D10Texture2D *texture,
+        const D3D10_TEXTURE2D_DESC *desc);
+
+/* Unmap everything the readers have finished with and park the textures for reuse.
+ * MUST be called from a presenting thread -- this is where the D3D work lives. */
+static void d2d_wic_gpu_drain_recycle(struct d2d_factory *factory)
+{
+    struct list done = LIST_INIT(done);
+    struct d2d_wic_gpu_handoff *h, *h2;
+    unsigned int freed = 0;
+    SIZE_T freed_bytes = 0;
+
+    EnterCriticalSection(&factory->cs);
+    list_move_tail(&done, &factory->wic_gpu_recycle);
+    LeaveCriticalSection(&factory->cs);
+
+    LIST_FOR_EACH_ENTRY_SAFE(h, h2, &done, struct d2d_wic_gpu_handoff, entry)
+    {
+        D3D10_TEXTURE2D_DESC desc;
+
+        list_remove(&h->entry);
+        ID3D10Texture2D_Unmap(h->texture, 0);
+        ID3D10Texture2D_GetDesc(h->texture, &desc);
+        d2d_wic_gpu_put_staging(factory, h->texture, &desc);
+        freed_bytes += h->bytes;
+        free(h);
+        ++freed;
+    }
+
+    if (freed)
+    {
+        EnterCriticalSection(&factory->cs);
+        factory->wic_gpu_outstanding -= freed;
+        factory->wic_gpu_outstanding_bytes -= freed_bytes;
+        LeaveCriticalSection(&factory->cs);
+    }
+}
+
+/* Park an unmapped staging texture for reuse, or release it if the pool is full.
+ * Matching is by shape AND device: a texture belongs to the device that made it, and the
+ * factory can host more than one. */
+static void d2d_wic_gpu_put_staging(struct d2d_factory *factory, ID3D10Texture2D *texture,
+        const D3D10_TEXTURE2D_DESC *desc)
+{
+    struct d2d_wic_staging *s;
+    BOOL parked = FALSE;
+
+    if ((s = malloc(sizeof(*s))))
+    {
+        s->texture = texture;
+        s->width = desc->Width;
+        s->height = desc->Height;
+        s->format = desc->Format;
+        ID3D10Texture2D_GetDevice(texture, &s->device);
+        ID3D10Device_Release(s->device);   /* borrowed; the texture keeps it alive */
+
+        EnterCriticalSection(&factory->cs);
+        if (factory->wic_staging_pool_size < D2D_WIC_STAGING_POOL_MAX)
+        {
+            list_add_head(&factory->wic_staging_pool, &s->entry);
+            ++factory->wic_staging_pool_size;
+            parked = TRUE;
+        }
+        LeaveCriticalSection(&factory->cs);
+
+        if (!parked) free(s);
+    }
+    if (!parked) ID3D10Texture2D_Release(texture);
+}
+
+/* A replacement staging texture for the one about to be given away. */
+static ID3D10Texture2D *d2d_wic_gpu_take_staging(struct d2d_factory *factory,
+        ID3D10Device *device, const D3D10_TEXTURE2D_DESC *desc)
+{
+    struct d2d_wic_staging *s, *found = NULL;
+    ID3D10Texture2D *texture = NULL;
+
+    EnterCriticalSection(&factory->cs);
+    LIST_FOR_EACH_ENTRY(s, &factory->wic_staging_pool, struct d2d_wic_staging, entry)
+    {
+        if (s->width == desc->Width && s->height == desc->Height
+                && s->format == desc->Format && s->device == device)
+        {
+            found = s;
+            break;
+        }
+    }
+    if (found)
+    {
+        list_remove(&found->entry);
+        --factory->wic_staging_pool_size;
+    }
+    LeaveCriticalSection(&factory->cs);
+
+    if (found)
+    {
+        texture = found->texture;
+        free(found);
+        InterlockedIncrement(&nwg_staging_hit);
+        return texture;
+    }
+
+    /* ID3D10Device1 derives from ID3D10Device, so the base interface is enough here. */
+    if (FAILED(ID3D10Device_CreateTexture2D(device, desc, NULL, &texture)))
+        texture = NULL;
+    else
+        InterlockedIncrement(&nwg_staging_new);
+    return texture;
+}
+
+void d2d_factory_drain_wic_gpu(struct d2d_factory *factory)
+{
+    struct d2d_wic_gpu_handoff *h, *h2;
+    struct d2d_wic_staging *s, *s2;
+
+    /* Guaranteed final totals. The periodic report is a running snapshot on a 512
+     * boundary, which on a short run is either nothing or an arbitrary prefix. */
+    if (TRACE_ON(nwicgpu))
+        TRACE_(nwicgpu)("neutron-wicgpu(d2d) FINAL: handed=%ld fallback_cap=%ld fallback_noiface=%ld "
+                "staging_reused=%ld staging_created=%ld\n",
+                nwg_handed, nwg_fallback_cap, nwg_fallback_noiface, nwg_staging_hit, nwg_staging_new);
+
+    LIST_FOR_EACH_ENTRY_SAFE(h, h2, &factory->wic_gpu_recycle, struct d2d_wic_gpu_handoff, entry)
+    {
+        list_remove(&h->entry);
+        ID3D10Texture2D_Unmap(h->texture, 0);
+        ID3D10Texture2D_Release(h->texture);
+        free(h);
+    }
+    LIST_FOR_EACH_ENTRY_SAFE(s, s2, &factory->wic_staging_pool, struct d2d_wic_staging, entry)
+    {
+        list_remove(&s->entry);
+        ID3D10Texture2D_Release(s->texture);
+        free(s);
+    }
+    factory->wic_staging_pool_size = 0;
+    factory->wic_gpu_outstanding = 0;
+}
+
+/* Drop any mapping still attached to this target's bitmap, without downloading it. Only
+ * correct immediately before overwriting the whole bitmap, which is what present does. */
+static void d2d_wic_render_target_detach(struct d2d_wic_render_target *rt)
+{
+    INeutronWicGpuBitmap *gpu;
+
+    if (FAILED(IWICBitmap_QueryInterface(rt->bitmap, &IID_INeutronWicGpuBitmap, (void **)&gpu)))
+        return;
+    INeutronWicGpuBitmap_SetSource(gpu, NULL, NULL);
+    INeutronWicGpuBitmap_Release(gpu);
+}
+
+/* Hand the freshly-mapped staging texture to the WIC bitmap. Returns TRUE when the
+ * bitmap took it, in which case the caller owes it a replacement texture and must NOT
+ * unmap. Every failure path leaves the caller free to do the ordinary download. */
+static BOOL d2d_wic_render_target_hand_off(struct d2d_wic_render_target *rt,
+        struct d2d_factory *factory, const D3D10_MAPPED_TEXTURE2D *mapped)
+{
+    INeutronWicGpuBitmap *gpu;
+    struct d2d_wic_gpu_handoff *h;
+    unsigned int outstanding;
+    SIZE_T bytes;
+    BOOL over;
+
+    if (FAILED(IWICBitmap_QueryInterface(rt->bitmap, &IID_INeutronWicGpuBitmap, (void **)&gpu)))
+    {
+        /* Not Wine's own windowscodecs, so there is nothing to hand it to. */
+        InterlockedIncrement(&nwg_fallback_noiface);
+        return FALSE;
+    }
+
+    bytes = (SIZE_T)mapped->RowPitch * rt->height;
+
+    EnterCriticalSection(&factory->cs);
+    outstanding = factory->wic_gpu_outstanding;
+    over = outstanding >= D2D_WIC_GPU_MAX_OUTSTANDING
+            || factory->wic_gpu_outstanding_bytes + bytes > neutron_wic_readthrough_bytes();
+    if (!over)
+    {
+        ++factory->wic_gpu_outstanding;
+        factory->wic_gpu_outstanding_bytes += bytes;
+        if (factory->wic_gpu_outstanding > nwg_peak_count)
+            nwg_peak_count = factory->wic_gpu_outstanding;
+        if (factory->wic_gpu_outstanding_bytes > nwg_peak_bytes)
+            nwg_peak_bytes = factory->wic_gpu_outstanding_bytes;
+    }
+    LeaveCriticalSection(&factory->cs);
+
+    if (over || !(h = malloc(sizeof(*h))))
+    {
+        if (!over)
+        {
+            EnterCriticalSection(&factory->cs);
+            --factory->wic_gpu_outstanding;
+            factory->wic_gpu_outstanding_bytes -= bytes;
+            LeaveCriticalSection(&factory->cs);
+        }
+        /* Reported every time, not on a sampling boundary. The periodic report is a
+         * running snapshot and a rare event that happens after the last boundary is
+         * simply invisible -- which is how the cap looked like it never fired. */
+        InterlockedIncrement(&nwg_fallback_cap);
+        if (TRACE_ON(nwicgpu))
+            TRACE_(nwicgpu)("neutron-wicgpu(d2d): CAP HIT, %u outstanding / %u MB -- falling "
+                    "back to the download (fallback_cap=%ld so far)\n", outstanding,
+                    (unsigned)(factory->wic_gpu_outstanding_bytes >> 20), nwg_fallback_cap);
+        INeutronWicGpuBitmap_Release(gpu);
+        return FALSE;
+    }
+
+    h->factory = factory;
+    h->factory_iface = rt->pool_factory;
+    ID2D1Factory1_AddRef(h->factory_iface);
+    h->texture = rt->readback_texture;    /* ownership moves to the bitmap */
+    h->data = mapped->pData;
+    h->row_pitch = mapped->RowPitch;
+    h->bytes = bytes;
+    h->width = rt->width;
+    h->height = rt->height;
+    h->bpp = rt->bpp;
+    list_init(&h->entry);
+
+    INeutronWicGpuBitmap_SetSource(gpu, &d2d_wic_gpu_ops, h);
+    INeutronWicGpuBitmap_Release(gpu);
+
+    InterlockedIncrement(&nwg_handed);
+    d2d_wic_gpu_report();
+    return TRUE;
+}
+
 static HRESULT d2d_wic_render_target_present(IUnknown *outer_unknown)
 {
     struct d2d_wic_render_target *render_target = impl_from_IUnknown(outer_unknown);
@@ -52,6 +436,61 @@ static HRESULT d2d_wic_render_target_present(IUnknown *outer_unknown)
     ID3D10Device_Release(device);
     ID3D10Resource_Release(src_resource);
 
+    /* NEUTRON WIC READ-THROUGH: map once and give the mapping to the bitmap instead of
+     * copying through it. Ordering matters -- the recycle queue is drained FIRST so this
+     * present can reuse a texture a reader has just finished with, and the map happens
+     * before the handoff so a failure leaves us on the ordinary path with nothing to
+     * unwind. */
+    if (neutron_wic_readthrough() && render_target->pool_factory)
+    {
+        struct d2d_factory *factory =
+                unsafe_impl_from_ID2D1Factory((ID2D1Factory *)render_target->pool_factory);
+
+        d2d_wic_gpu_drain_recycle(factory);
+
+        if (SUCCEEDED(ID3D10Texture2D_Map(render_target->readback_texture, 0,
+                D3D10_MAP_READ, 0, &mapped_texture)))
+        {
+            D3D10_TEXTURE2D_DESC desc;
+            ID3D10Texture2D *replacement;
+            ID3D10Device *dev;
+
+            ID3D10Texture2D_GetDesc(render_target->readback_texture, &desc);
+            ID3D10Texture2D_GetDevice(render_target->readback_texture, &dev);
+
+            /* Secure the replacement BEFORE giving the texture away. Handing off first
+             * and then discovering we cannot replace it would mean prising the pixels
+             * back out of a bitmap that already owns them -- an unwind path with no
+             * good outcome. This way every failure simply falls through to the copy. */
+            replacement = d2d_wic_gpu_take_staging(factory, dev, &desc);
+            ID3D10Device_Release(dev);
+
+            /* Whatever happens below, this present is about to overwrite every pixel of
+             * the bitmap, so any mapping still attached to it is dead. Detach it WITHOUT
+             * materialising -- otherwise the fallback path's Lock(WRITE) faithfully
+             * downloads the previous frame into a buffer we immediately paint over.
+             * That wasted a full copy on every fallback, on top of the copy the fallback
+             * already does, and made the fix cost more than it saved the moment the cap
+             * was reached. */
+            d2d_wic_render_target_detach(render_target);
+
+            if (replacement)
+            {
+                if (d2d_wic_render_target_hand_off(render_target, factory, &mapped_texture))
+                {
+                    /* The bitmap owns the old texture and its mapping now. This target --
+                     * which the pool may hand to a different bitmap at any moment -- draws
+                     * into the replacement, so it can never write over pixels somebody is
+                     * still reading. */
+                    render_target->readback_texture = replacement;
+                    goto end;
+                }
+                d2d_wic_gpu_put_staging(factory, replacement, &desc);
+            }
+            ID3D10Texture2D_Unmap(render_target->readback_texture, 0);
+        }
+    }
+
     dst_rect.X = 0;
     dst_rect.Y = 0;
     dst_rect.Width = render_target->width;
diff --git a/dlls/windowscodecs/bitmap.c b/dlls/windowscodecs/bitmap.c
index e7d8b27d94a..1d5eca90887 100644
--- a/dlls/windowscodecs/bitmap.c
+++ b/dlls/windowscodecs/bitmap.c
@@ -33,7 +33,10 @@
